  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk that alignment research findings remain unavailable for timely scrutiny by other researchers, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "The proposal is to add structural support to research fellowships that encourages earlier, more frequent sharing of results, including intermediate outputs. The argument is that academic incentives can push fellows toward paper-like products rather than shorter posts, reducing how much research they communicate during the fellowship. This delays ideas reaching the wider community for discussion, evaluation, criticism, and improvement. Encouraging fellows to release intermediate work directly is intended to shorten this delay and allow useful scrutiny and refinement sooner.",
  "spans": [
    "Among others, we suspect that academic incentives contributed to a tendency across the cohort to sometimes aim for paper-like outputs over, e.g., forum posts, which in turn leads to a reduced volume of communicable research output within the period of the fellowship (see the point about concrete output above).",
    "This can cause delays in ideas reaching and being discussed/evaluated/criticized/improved upon by the larger epistemic community.",
    "Going forward, we will be more cognizant of the relevant incentive landscapes, and, in particular, add structures to the program design encouraging fellows to release (even if intermediary) outputs directly as well."
  ]
}
